  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 305 AM CDT Tue Oct 20 2026\n\nUpper low continues to deepen off the Pacific coast with southwest \nflow aloft covering the Rockies and Plains. VAD wind profilers are \nindicative of a weaker shortwave in the OK/TX panhandle vicinity. A \nweak area of sfc low pressure is noted in the lee of the Rockies \nwith weak isentropic ascent in OK and south central KS. In response \nto this, mid/high clouds are moving into the region with some light \nreturns on radar, but only one observation of rain reaching the \nground thus far in Wichita. Isentropic lift remains weak through the \nmorning, but looks to increase somewhat into the afternoon with \nbetter upper support this evening and overnight from the passing \nshortwave. Overall, limited moisture through the column may mean \nless actual rainfall compared to what is shown on CAMs through the \nday today and overnight tonight. The thinking is that there is still \nenough to support a few light showers or sprinkles, which will \nlikely be hit or miss over the next 24 hours, so have gone with a \nbroad mention of this area-wide through early Wednesday morning. \nOtherwise, expect increasing cloud cover with enough southerly wind \nto keep high temperatures around 70 degrees.\n\nOnce the shortwave passes Wednesday morning, the upper ridge axis \nbuilds and inches closer to the area while the Pacific trough slowly \nmoves onto the coast. This favors warmer temperatures for the middle \nand end of the work week, bringing highs to the mid and upper 70s \nwhile lows slowly climb through the 50s each morning. Models still \ndiverge on their evolution of the western trough by the weekend, \nwhich results in differences in timing of the sfc front and \nassociated rain chances. Some solutions are trying to stall the \nfront nearby, or potentially still northwest of the area through the \nweekend and delay it until Monday. This leads to overall low \nconfidence, low probability rain chances mainly in western portions \nof the area Saturday, which spread across the area into Monday. \nTemperatures overall trend a bit cooler (closer to seasonal norms) \ninto early next week, but it remains to be seen exactly when we see \nthat cool-down as spreads become larger Sunday and beyond.",
